// db/user.lite — what this shop says about a model it did not write.
//
// ─── Why this is a second file ────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `db/schema.lite` is read by TWO things and they see different documents. The
// API assembles it in memory with auth's fragments appended (api/src/core/db.ts,
// `authSchemaFragments`), so `User` is there. The BROWSER build reads the file
// alone — sierra's schema plugin, off disk — and `User` is not.
//
// So `extend model User` cannot live in that file. Put there it parses for the
// API and refuses for the browser, by name: *no model 'User' is declared or
// imported*. The schema registry then ships EMPTY, every `createResource` falls
// back to a bare `make()`, and the first symptom is a product page that renders
// no buy button. The refusal is correct; the file was the wrong one.
//
// It is not `import`ed by schema.lite either, for the same reason — an import
// is resolved by both readers. api/src/core/db.ts reads it beside auth's own
// fragments, which is the one place where both halves exist.

// ─── Who is staff ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A verified shopper and a member of staff both grade USER(4). They must: the
// ladder answers *what kind of caller* — a stranger, a half-registered one, a
// real one, an administrator — and *is this person one of ours* is not on that
// axis at all. Saying it with a level means either giving staff level 5, which
// is the delete-and-refund tier, or leaving shoppers at 1 for ever, which makes
// their own order history unreadable to them.
//
// It cannot be `role` either, and that is the sharp bit: auth's `role` defaults
// to `"user"`, so **every account the storefront registers arrives with the
// same role the shop's own staff have**. A policy reading `auth().role ==
// 'user'` as *staff* would hand every shopper the whole order book.
//
// It reaches `auth()` through `sessionFields` in api/src/app.ts — the one route
// an app's own User columns take onto the SessionContext.
extend model User {
  isStaff Boolean @default(false) @allow('write', auth().isAdmin)
}

// ─── Who may read the roster ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// auth ships `User` at `@@gate("4.4.4.5")` and NO read policy, which is the
// right default for an app whose only people are its own staff: read at USER(4)
// so an app's own screens can list them.
//
// This shop is not that app. A shopper registers on the public storefront and
// grades USER(4) exactly as a member of staff does — that is the whole point of
// `isStaff` — so the shipped default hands every shopper the roster: every
// address, every role, every colleague. A gate cannot say this, because a gate
// is per MODEL and the difference here is per ROW.
//
// So the policy is the second half of the same sentence `isStaff` started. A
// person always reads their own row, which is what the account screen needs;
// staff read everybody, which is what the users screen needs. Nobody else reads
// anything, and they read it as an EMPTY LIST with a 200 — a policy filters
// where a gate refuses (Invariant 6).
//
// It costs auth nothing: every write this package makes goes through
// asSystem(), which is above the ladder and above this.
extend model User {
  @@allow('read', id == auth().id || auth().isStaff)

  // ─── Who may add somebody ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // auth ships create at USER(4), which is right for the model it ships: the
  // only thing that reaches it is `POST /auth/register`, and registration by
  // definition happens before anybody is anybody. Once an app puts a `users`
  // service on the same model there is a SECOND door, and at 4 every signed-in
  // shopper could walk through it — inventing accounts on a shop they merely
  // buy from.
  //
  // The gate cannot say so, and not for a subtle reason: `@@gate` is
  // single-valued, so an `extend model` may not restate it (the parser refuses
  // a second answer by name). A policy is the mechanism that IS repeatable, and
  // create is the one operation where a policy throws rather than filters —
  // there is no stored row to hide, the payload IS the row. So a shopper gets a
  // 403 that names the model, which is the correct answer to *may I* and is
  // exactly what a filtered empty list would fail to say.
  //
  // It costs registration nothing. Every write @frontierjs/auth makes goes
  // through `asSystem()`, which is above the ladder and above this — so
  // `/auth/register` and `db/seed.ts` are unaffected, and the only caller this
  // grades is a request to the app's own `users` service.
  @@allow('create', auth().isAdmin)
}
